# Paths vendormark may skip. One glob per line.
#
# The rule it enforces is right: a site-generator vendor's name in a tracked path
# is a URL the browser requests, so shipping one puts that vendor's branding in
# our page. Almost every hit is exactly that, and this file should stay almost
# empty.
#
# products/mcp_analytics/frontend/harness-logos/ is the exception, and it is the
# inverse case. That directory is a REGISTRY OF OTHER PEOPLE'S MARKS — claude,
# cursor, vscode, notion, replit, windsurf, grok, linear and eleven more — which
# the MCP dashboard draws beside a row to say which client made the call.
# harnessRegistry.ts imports each one by filename and the backend maps the
# reported client name to it, so the mark is DATA, the way a browser icon is data
# in a web-analytics table. Fifteen of those files are unremarkable to this gate
# and one is not, purely because `lovable` happens to sit on the vendor list —
# the name is the same, the meaning is opposite.
#
# The gate's own remedy does not fit here either: it says to replace the asset
# with the real brand mark, and the real brand mark for a row about Lovable IS
# Lovable's. Putting ours there would mislabel the data.
#
# Scoped to the logo directory, so a genuine vendor asset anywhere else in
# mcp_analytics is still caught.
products/mcp_analytics/frontend/harness-logos/*
